From 95dbd47f1e3e8874b5b761e9ae03cb86ef46cce6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Proton SpeedHack <speedhack@invalid>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 11:06:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll, server: Add Proton SpeedHack clock scaling.

Accelerate the clocks seen by applications by a global, per-prefix
multiplier, based on the idea of LtSquigs' Proton SpeedHack for
Proton 8.0 (https://github.com/LtSquigs/wine, branch proton_8.0).

The wineserver owns the speed state.  It re-anchors the accelerated
clock on every change so that it stays continuous, and publishes the
anchors under a sequence lock in the unused tail of the
KUSER_SHARED_DATA page.  ntdll scales its own high resolution clock
reads with them, so NtQueryPerformanceCounter keeps full resolution
(Proton 8.0's version derived it from the 16 ms InterruptTime).

Accelerated:  NtQueryPerformanceCounter (QueryPerformanceCounter,
              timeGetTime), InterruptTime and TickCount in
              KUSER_SHARED_DATA (GetTickCount[64], QueryInterruptTime,
              QueryUnbiasedInterruptTime) and server message times.
Unchanged:    system (wall clock) time, wait timeouts, sleeps and
              server timers, which keep running on the real monotonic
              clock.  ntdll uses the real clock wherever it exchanges
              monotonic times with the wineserver.

The speed is set with the Wine-private NtSetSystemInformation class
SystemWineSpeedhackInformation (0x5348).  As long as the speed was
never changed all clocks are bit-for-bit identical to upstream.
---
